<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Chapter 2</a> &gt; Level 15</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 15 Walkthrough: Fashion Store</h1>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from a recorded playthrough — a 4:09 capture pulled apart at one frame per second, plus a 1080p upload of the same run used to re-read the small print on the bill and the receipt. The bill total, the four receipt lines and the five-digit code were each read twice from separate frames and agree. Two things the footage does not settle are flagged on this page rather than smoothed over: the combination the four-wheel dial actually accepts, and the rule the shoe wall is following. Everything below is what the frames show.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={2} current={15} variant="jump" label="Chapter 2 · No Honor Among Thieves" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>The Fashion Store ends at the front door, and the door code is <b>30015</b>. It comes from the register, not from a wall: pay the bill, scan the printed receipt, and read the code off the item the game hands you. Finish worth <b>+5 coins</b> in 3:57.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Tap the <b>register counter</b> → <b>Handwritten bill</b> (3:15).</li>
        <li>Open the bag and <b>Examine</b> the bill: 25,50 / 299,00 / 32,99 / 6,50, boxed total <b>363,99</b>.</li>
        <li>Go back to the register and tap the monitor → numeric keypad.</li>
        <li>Type <b>363,99</b> — with the comma — and press the green <b>ENTER</b>.</li>
        <li>Take the <b>Receipt</b> the printer spits out, select <b>Barcode reader with cable</b> in the bag, press <b>Use</b>, and tap the receipt.</li>
        <li>The scan returns a <b>Code</b> item reading <b>30015</b>.</li>
        <li>Tap the keypad beside the shop's <b>double doors</b> and enter <b>30015</b> → results screen, <b>+5 coins</b>, 3:57.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
            <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="-mxXZcwRnmo" data-title="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 15 Fashion Store walkthrough"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 15 Fashion Store walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/-mxXZcwRnmo/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 15 Fashion Store walkthrough" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/-mxXZcwRnmo/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-hero-fashion-store.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 2 Level 15 Fashion Store: two-floor boutique with a glass display window, three dressed mannequins, a shoe wall of coloured heels, a register counter and a staircase to the upper level" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The Fashion Store is a boutique with two floors and no corridor, no lift and no second building. You start outside the glass, you walk in through a pair of double doors under a green EXIT sign, and every object you will ever need is inside the same box of walls. That is the whole map. It is also why the room has a reputation for being longer than it is: there is no door you cannot see from the entrance, so there is nothing to narrow your search, and the shop is dense with things that look like puzzles.</p>
    <p>They mostly are puzzles, which is the trap. The boutique is stuffed with mini-games — a chest with four coloured pads on its lid, a row of framed outfit photographs, a four-wheel dial that starts on 0-0-0-0, a wall of high heels with green arrows beside it, a panel with four round buttons, a sixteen-tile wooden slider, a cabinet with a padlock. Each one is a real interaction, each one answers back, and none of them is the finish line. The level ends at the front door with a keypad code, and that code is downstream of the cash register: a bill on the counter, a total you have to type, a receipt the register prints, and a barcode on that receipt that a reader in your bag turns into five digits.</p>
    <p>So the Fashion Store has two spines. The first is the register chain, and it is compulsory: bill → total → receipt → scan → code → door. The second is the shop floor itself, a set of display puzzles that hand you items and keep the room busy. Most players spend their first ten minutes on the second spine, because the shoe wall is enormous, lit like a shop window and clearly interactive, while the bill is a small pale rectangle sitting flat on a counter you have already walked past twice.</p>
    <p>The recording finishes in 3:57 of level time and 4:09 of footage, with an efficiency bar of nine out of ten. Nothing in the room is timed, there is no fail state, and no puzzle can be made unsolvable — you can spill every item out of the bag and the game will still let you pick it back up. The only thing that stretches this level is the order you do it in.</p>

    <h2>The shop, floor by floor<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>Ground floor.</b> The entrance is on the far left of the box: two glazed doors with dark frames, an EXIT sign above them, and a low console of shoes and handbags against the wall beside them. The front of the shop is a full-height glass window with two dressed mannequins in it — a standing figure in a yellow top, brown shorts and white heels, and a seated figure in a teal top, dark trousers, a wide-brim hat and white boots — arranged around a round tufted ottoman. Behind the window the floor opens up: a men's rail of hanging shirts and jackets on the left, a staircase with blue risers climbing to the upper level beside the entrance, a folded-clothes table in the middle, and the register counter at the back with a monitor, a receipt printer, a barcode scanner on a stand, a cash drawer, magazines and a tape dispenser.</p>
    <p>To the right of the register, a wall display holds several pairs of high-heeled shoes in bright colours — blue, green, yellow, pink — on individual shelves, with small green arrows beside them. Further right, hanging rails of blue, black, yellow and plaid shirts run along the wall with folded stock underneath, and a white tiered pedestal in the foreground carries handbags and a card reading 30%. A stack of vintage suitcases fills the corner behind the men's rail.</p>
    <p><b>Upper floor.</b> The staircase lands you in a smaller, softer room: a red dress on a mannequin against a window, a tall mirror, a counter with a glowing pink-violet top, a rack of garments on the back wall and a fitting area with blue curtains. The chest with the four coloured pads sits up here on a shelf, and so does a good deal of the shop's loose stock. The two floors share one camera and one bag, so anything you pick up upstairs is available downstairs immediately — there is no need to carry items between floors by hand.</p>
    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-window-display.jpg" alt="The Fashion Store seen from the front: glass display window with a standing and a seated mannequin around a tufted ottoman, double entrance doors with an EXIT sign on the left, staircase, men's rail and the shoe wall on the right" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:02</span>
      <strong>Read the shop from the doorway before you tap anything</strong>
      <p>The first frame after the title card gives you the entire level in one isometric shot. Find three things and you have the map: the double doors on the left with the green EXIT sign, the staircase immediately to their right, and the register counter at the back with a monitor and a small printer on it. Everything else in the room is either stock or a side puzzle. The doors are the finish, the stairs are the second floor, and the counter is the only place that generates a number.</p>
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=2s">0:02</a></p>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:13</span>
      <strong>The men's rail is scenery, not a puzzle</strong>
      <p>The camera drops onto the hanging rail on the left wall: blue striped shirts, a pale suit, a dark blazer, all on a wooden rail with folded stock on the shelf above. It looks like it should hide something, and in other Rooms &amp; Exits rooms a rail like this often does. Here it does not. Our recording taps along it, gets nothing, and moves on — the rail exists to make the shop read as a shop. Spend ten seconds on it and leave.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-mens-rail.jpg" alt="Close-up of the men's clothing rail in the Fashion Store: blue striped shirts, a pale suit and a dark blazer hanging on a wooden rail with folded stock above" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=13s">0:13</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:25</span>
      <strong>Find the register counter and remember where it is</strong>
      <p>This is the most important visit in the level and it looks like the least important. The counter holds a flat monitor on a white stand, a black cash drawer with a keyhole in its face, a small black receipt printer to the left of the drawer, a barcode scanner on a stand to the right with a coiled cable, an open magazine, a tape dispenser and a stack of notebooks behind. The monitor screen is showing a bare numeric keypad. You will come back here three times — once to collect the bill, once to pay it, once to deal with the receipt — so fix its position in your head now rather than hunting for it later.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-register-counter.jpg" alt="The Fashion Store register counter: monitor showing a numeric keypad, cash drawer with keyhole, receipt printer, barcode scanner on a stand, magazine and tape dispenser" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=25s">0:25</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:37</span>
      <strong>Climb to the upper floor and clear it in one sweep</strong>
      <p>The stairs take you to the boutique's second level: a red dress on a mannequin by the window, a tall mirror, a counter with a pink-violet glowing top, a rack of garments along the back wall and blue-curtained fitting rooms. Do a full lap before you touch anything, because the bag is shared between floors and there is no reason to walk back up. Two things up here are worth your time: the chest on the shelf with the four coloured pads on its lid, and the framed photographs on the wall. Everything else is stock.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-upper-boutique.jpg" alt="Upper floor of the Fashion Store: red dress on a mannequin beside a window, tall mirror, counter with a glowing pink top and a rack of garments" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=37s">0:37</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 0:57</span>
      <strong>The chest with four coloured pads</strong>
      <p>A wooden chest sits on a shelf upstairs with four square pads set into its lid. Tapping a pad lights it a different colour — purple, then red, then a pale blue in our frames — and once all four have been pressed the lid swings open and the game hands you whatever was inside. This is a sequence toy, not a code: there is no wrong order and nothing to read, so press all four and take the item. Our frames never give a legible label for the contents, so this page does not name it.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-wooden-chest.jpg" alt="Wooden chest on a shelf in the Fashion Store with four square pads set into its lid, lit in different colours after being pressed" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=57s">0:57</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:05</span>
      <strong>The fitting rooms are a dead end — check and move on</strong>
      <p>The blue-curtained fitting area and the mirror behind it are the most "escape room" looking objects on the upper floor, and they are empty. There is no hidden catch, no gap behind the curtain, no code written in the mirror frame. In our recording the camera sweeps them twice and the player never interacts. If you are the kind of player who needs to be told, this is the telling: the Fashion Store's secrets are all on the ground floor and in the bag interface, not up here.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-fitting-rooms.jpg" alt="The Fashion Store's fitting area: blue curtains, a mirror and a rack of garments on the upper floor, with the red dress mannequin at the edge of the frame" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=65s">1:05</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:19</span>
      <strong>The framed outfit photographs over the shoe console</strong>
      <p>A row of framed photographs of dressed figures hangs on the wall above a console of shoes and handbags, near the men's rail. These are the shop's styling reference — each frame shows a complete outfit. Our recording looks at them immediately before working the shoe wall, which is the natural reading: the photographs tell you which shoes belong with which look. That link is an inference, not something the game states, so treat the photographs as the reference for the shoe display rather than as a code. Nothing in the frames carries a number.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-outfit-photos.jpg" alt="Framed photographs of dressed mannequin outfits hanging in a row above a console of shoes and handbags in the Fashion Store" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=79s">1:19</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 1:34</span>
      <strong>The four-wheel dial and the paper slip</strong>
      <p>On a shelf unit on the ground floor there is a four-wheel combination dial that reads 0-0-0-0 when you first find it, and separately a small slip of paper that goes into the bag as "Piece of paper" with four letters on it. They are the room's most obviously-coded objects and the pair most likely to be connected — but this is exactly where the footage fails us. We could not read the dial's accepted combination twice from independent frames, so this page prints no number for it. If your copy of the puzzle needs it, the slip in your own bag is the authoritative version; do not trust a number from a video's on-screen annotation.</p>
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=94s">1:34</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:03</span>
      <strong>The sixteen-tile wooden panel</strong>
      <p>A rectangular panel framed with white corner brackets holds a four-by-four grid of square tiles, each split diagonally into two triangles in three shades of brown and beige, with a small dark dot in the centre of some tiles. Turning a tile rotates its two triangles. The target is a continuous pattern across the grid rather than a picture, and the dots mark the tiles that have to end up in a specific orientation. Clearing it opens a shallow compartment behind the panel; our frames show a hairdryer sitting in it, and it goes straight into the bag.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-tile-panel.jpg" alt="The Fashion Store's four-by-four wooden tile panel: sixteen square tiles split diagonally into brown and beige triangles, some with a dark dot, framed by white corner brackets" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=123s">2:03</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 2:39</span>
      <strong>The shoe wall with the green arrows</strong>
      <p>To the right of the register, a wall of individual shelves holds high-heeled shoes in blue, green, yellow and pink, and small green arrows sit between the shelves. Tapping an arrow moves a pair, so the display is a sorting toy: the shop wants its stock arranged, and the photographs on the other wall are the reference for how. Our recording works along the shelves from the top, moving pairs and picking up the loose shoes that are lying around the shop floor — they arrive in the bag simply as "Shoes". We could not confirm the exact winning arrangement twice, so this page describes the mechanism rather than printing a layout.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-shoe-wall.jpg" alt="The Fashion Store's shoe wall: individual shelves holding high-heeled shoes in blue, green, yellow and pink with small green arrows between them" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=159s">2:39</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 2:52</span>
      <strong>The button panel: one square pad, four round buttons</strong>
      <p>A wall panel holds one square button with a red dot above four square modules, each carrying a round coloured button — red, blue, yellow and green — with two small black dots beside it. The bag shows a matching "Button" item whose icon is four coloured segments with a 3/4 counter, which is the game telling you it is a collect-and-fit object rather than a code. Press the pads you have, watch which modules light, and keep the panel open while you sweep the floor: the pieces you are missing are lying in the shop.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-button-panel.jpg" alt="Wall panel in the Fashion Store with one square button carrying a red dot above four modules with round red, blue, yellow and green buttons" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=172s">2:52</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 3:15</span>
      <strong>Pick the handwritten bill off the register counter</strong>
      <p>Back at the counter, tap the surface next to the printer and the game hands you the item labelled <b>Handwritten bill</b>. It is a small pale rectangle lying flat, easy to mistake for part of the counter's decoration, and it is the single most important pickup in the room. Everything that follows — the keypad, the receipt, the scan, the code, the door — is downstream of this one tap. If you have been stuck in the Fashion Store for twenty minutes, this is almost certainly the thing you have not done.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-handwritten-bill.jpg" alt="Inventory screen in the Fashion Store with the Handwritten bill selected and enlarged, showing four handwritten prices and a boxed total" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=195s">3:15</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 13 · 3:19</span>
      <strong>Read the bill: four prices and a boxed total</strong>
      <p>Open the bag and press <b>Examine</b>. The bill is a handwritten column of four prices with the sum boxed underneath: <b>25,50</b>, <b>299,00</b>, <b>32,99</b>, <b>6,50</b>, and a boxed <b>363,99</b>. Add them and the four figures land on 363,99 exactly, which is the check that matters — this is a handwriting puzzle, the nines have a long tail that reads as a seven at thumbnail size, and the sum is what tells you which one you are looking at. Write the total down before you close the bag; the register wants it character for character.</p>
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=199s">3:19</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 14 · 3:25</span>
      <strong>Type 363,99 into the register and press ENTER</strong>
      <p>Tap the register monitor and the screen becomes a plain numeric keypad: digits 0 to 9, a red <b>CLEAR</b>, a red <b>DEL</b>, a green <b>ENTER</b>, and an amount field along the top. Type <b>363,99</b> — the bill is written in the comma decimal style and the amount has to match it. Use CLEAR rather than DEL if a digit goes in wrong: DEL removes one character at a time, and a stray figure left in front of the amount turns 363,99 into something the register will not take. Check the field, then press ENTER.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-register-keypad.jpg" alt="The Fashion Store register monitor showing a numeric keypad with digits, a red CLEAR, a red DEL and a green ENTER button" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=205s">3:25</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 15 · 3:39</span>
      <strong>Take the receipt and scan it with the barcode reader</strong>
      <p>The printer produces a receipt and it lands in your bag as <b>Receipt</b>. Read it once and you will see the same four lines the bill gave you — a T-shirt at 25.50, a watch at 299.00, a pair of trousers at 32.99 and socks at 6.50, a total of 363.99, cash 400.00, change 36.01, a card ending 7211 and an approval number 192107. Under the THANK YOU line there is a barcode. That barcode is the point. Select <b>Barcode reader with cable</b> in the bag, press <b>Use</b>, and tap the receipt: the view switches to a close-up of the paper inside white corner brackets, a red scan line sweeps across it, and the reader does its job.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-scan-receipt.jpg" alt="The receipt from the Fashion Store register being scanned: white corner brackets around the paper, a red scanner line across the middle and a barcode at the bottom" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=219s">3:39</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 16 · 3:48</span>
      <strong>The scan returns the code: 30015</strong>
      <p>A white pop-up prints <b>30015</b> and the game drops a <b>Code</b> item into the bag with those five digits on it. That is the whole reveal — no wall writing, no arithmetic, no letter-to-number conversion. It is also why the Fashion Store is hard to spoil by searching the room: the code does not exist in the world until the register has produced a receipt and the reader has been run over it. If a video shows you a five-digit door code for this level, this is where it came from.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-code-30015.jpg" alt="White pop-up in the Fashion Store reading 30015 above the label Code, with the item slot visible on the right" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=228s">3:48</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 17 · 3:53</span>
      <strong>Enter 30015 at the door and finish</strong>
      <p>Walk back to the double doors under the EXIT sign. The keypad is mounted on the door beside the handle: a gold twelve-key pad with 1 to 9 in three rows and the 0 beneath the 8, and two small indicator lamps below the zero — red on the left, green on the right. Tap it, type <b>30015</b>, and the green lamp answers. The doors release, the camera pulls back to the isometric view of the whole shop, and the results screen comes up. There is no key item and no final Combine — the code alone is the exit.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-door-keypad.jpg" alt="The keypad beside the Fashion Store's double entrance doors, with red and green indicator lamps and digits one to nine" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=233s">3:53</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 18 · 4:05</span>
      <strong>The results screen</strong>
      <p>The Fashion Store pays <b>+5 coins</b> with an efficiency bar of nine segments out of ten, and the recorded run finishes at <b>Time 3:57</b>. The Piggy Bank panel underneath is the game's own upsell — it fills as you complete levels and asks to be bought — and nothing on it affects the room. Five coins is a small payout for a two-floor level, which is worth knowing if you are farming currency: this is not the room to replay for coins.</p>
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l15-level-complete.jpg" alt="Level completed screen for the Fashion Store: plus five coins, an efficiency bar with nine of ten segments filled, time 3:57 and the Piggy Bank panel" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <p class="ts">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mxXZcwRnmo&t=245s">4:05</a></p>
    </div>

    <h2>Why the register chain is the whole level<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Strip out every display puzzle and the Fashion Store is a five-link chain. A bill on the counter carries a total. The register's keypad accepts that total. When it does, the receipt printer fires and the receipt goes into your bag. The receipt carries a barcode, and the barcode reader in your bag converts it into a five-digit code. The keypad beside the front doors accepts that code. Nothing else in the room produces a number that a lock will take, and no lock in the room can be opened without something the chain has already produced.</p>
    <p>The chain's weak link is the first one, and it fails for a very ordinary reason: the bill does not look like a puzzle object. Rooms &amp; Exits usually marks its pickups by making them odd — a note pinned at an angle, a sheet sticking out of a drawer, a card face-up on a table. This one is a plain pale rectangle lying flat on a counter, roughly the same tone as the counter's surface, and the camera only shows it clearly when you are already zoomed in on the register. Our recording only notices it at 3:15, after nearly three minutes of working the shop floor, and that is a player who knew it was there.</p>
    <p>The second link is the formatting trap. The bill is written the European way: 25,50 / 299,00 / 32,99 / 6,50 / 363,99. If you read the four figures and then type them into the register the way you would write them in a spreadsheet — 363.99 with a full stop — you are entering a string the keypad is not laid out for. The register screen is a plain numeric pad with a red CLEAR, a red DEL and a green ENTER; type the amount the way the paper writes it, with the comma. If you fumble a digit, use CLEAR and start the amount again rather than DEL, which only walks back one character.</p>
    <p>The third link is the one people miss even after they have paid. The receipt is not a receipt in the ordinary sense — it is the level's code carrier, and the code is printed as a barcode under the THANK YOU line rather than written out. You cannot read the code off the paper by eye. You have to open the bag, select <b>Barcode reader with cable</b>, press <b>Use</b>, and then tap the receipt. Only then does the game run the scan animation — the paper inside white corner brackets, a red line sweeping across it — and only then does the five-digit pop-up appear. Players who take the receipt, examine it, see a normal shop receipt, and conclude the level has glitched are one Use press away from the answer.</p>
    <p>The final link is anticlimactic on purpose. The door keypad takes the five digits, the green lamp lights, the camera pulls back and the results screen appears. There is no key to combine, no final item to fit, no second door. The Fashion Store is one of the Chapter 2 rooms where the last action is the smallest one, which is why a run that knows the chain can finish in under four minutes while a run that does not can circle the shoe wall for twenty.</p>

    <div class="warn">
      <p><b>What this page does not claim.</b> Two things in the Fashion Store could not be pinned down twice from independent frames, and they are listed here rather than buried in the text above.</p>
      <p><b>1. The four-wheel dial's combination.</b> The dial reads 0-0-0-0 on first sight, and a paper slip with four letters goes into the bag. We saw the dial, we saw the slip, but we never got two agreeing reads of the number the dial accepts. One widely-shared upload of this level carries a four-digit number burned onto the frame as an annotation; we could not confirm that number is in the game rather than in the video's edit, so this page prints none. Use the slip in your own bag.</p>
      <p><b>2. The shoe wall's winning arrangement.</b> The green arrows move pairs and the framed photographs are the obvious reference, but no frame in either upload shows the completed display long enough to read the layout twice. The mechanism is described above; the arrangement is not.</p>
      <p>Everything else on this page — the bill total, the four receipt lines, the five-digit code, the reward, the time — was read twice and agrees.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>They never find the bill.</b> It is on the register counter, flat and pale, and it is the single pickup that unlocks the entire chain. If the register keypad is inert for you, you are missing this and nothing else. Tap the counter surface next to the receipt printer.</li>
      <li><b>They type 363.99 instead of 363,99.</b> The bill is written with commas and the register wants the same style. The amount field is at the top of the screen and it is easy to type a full stop out of habit from every other game you have played.</li>
      <li><b>They never scan the receipt.</b> The receipt goes into the bag and looks finished. The code only exists after the Barcode reader has been used on it, and the reader has to be selected in the bag first — tapping the receipt with nothing selected does nothing.</li>
      <li><b>They treat the shoe wall as the main puzzle.</b> It is the biggest, brightest, most obviously interactive object in the room, and it is a side puzzle. Our recording spends over a minute on it and the finish does not depend on it.</li>
      <li><b>They hunt for the code on the walls.</b> There is no five-digit number painted anywhere in this shop. The code is generated by the register and handed to you as an item, which is why searching the room for it is hopeless.</li>
      <li><b>They read the bill's 9s as 7s.</b> At thumbnail size, the handwritten 32,99 and 363,99 both look like 32,97 and 363,97. The four line items are the check: 25,50 + 299,00 + 32,99 + 6,50 = 363,99, and the printed receipt confirms the same figures once the transaction goes through.</li>
      <li><b>They try to carry items between floors.</b> There is no carrying. The bag is shared across the whole level, so anything picked up upstairs is available at the register downstairs without a trip back.</li>
    </ul>

